// An `exists`-bodied spec whose claim is about a linked external. `double` has
// no body here — it arrives from `spec_linked_extern_mathlib.inf`, spliced in by
// the static-merge linker under the name section symbol `mathlib::double`.
//
// `rocq_exists_spec.inf` is the corpus producer of the `ValidExistsSpec`
// grammar, but every application in its payload names a function from its own
// translation unit. This is the only fixture whose reachability payload applies
// a body the compiler never emitted: the merged ordinal shows up inside the
// `reach_payload`, where a single-file fixture can never put it.
//
// Shaped the way it is because the corpus runs parse → type-check → codegen
// with NO analysis, so every constraint is carried by the fixture itself:
// the obligation is non-vacuous (the `assert` states a real property, so
// nothing collapses to the vacuous `HA_true` — P010 rejects the collapse); and
// the body neither declares a return type nor contains `return` (the verifier
// reduces the retained body without an enclosing activation frame, so a
// `return` could never take a step — the pre-scan rejects both shapes).
//
// The obligation must also be TRUE, and the verifier's contract quantifies the
// entry parameters universally: `exists_spec_holds_at` demands a successful
// choice vector for EVERY typed entry value. The body is therefore
// parameterless, which discharges that quantifier trivially — an `assume` over
// an entry parameter cannot carve out entries, it can only falsify the spec at
// the ones it rejects. The single filter pins the named choice to `3`, and
// `double(3)` is `6`, so the surviving choice satisfies the claim.
//
// No data segment: the merged module built from this one is compiled by `coqc`,
// and the corpus rejects a data segment in any module it gates.

external fn double(x: i32) -> i32;
use { double } from mathlib;

pub fn twice(x: i32) -> i32 {
  return double(x);
}

spec ReachableExtern {
  fn reaches_six() exists {
    let x: i32 = @;
    assume {
      assert(x == 3);
    }
    assert(double(x) == 6);
  }
}
